                        <field name="dc_description">Printed by William A. Mercein, 240 Pearl Street. -- Dedication "to the Honl. James Kent, late Chancellor of the state of New York", signed "J. Buchanan, New York, 1st August, 1834". -- Includes: preface ; letter addressed to Sir John Colborne, K.C.B. &c., dated 4th March 1833 ; extracts from letters to Sir Robert Peel (1828)  and to the Right Honourable Edward G.S. Stanley (December 1833) ; extracts from reports in relation to the police of the state of New York, and regarding poor laws of the state of New York (addresed to Viscount Palmerston, 1853) ; section on "management of poor in Connecticut" ; rules and regulations; and estimates. --  On front cover: "Extracts from Mr. Buchanan's letters and reports, as to state prisons, crime, poor laws, democracy, universal suffrage, education, reformation of criminals in the state of New York, with a proposition to provide for the surplus working population of England, plan and estimate:- to which is added, suggestions for upholding the property of the church, preserving vested rights, exonerating dissenters from contributing thereto, and applying the tythes for the support of the poor". --  Handwritten inscriptions on front-cover: "[?] Buchanan's letter, 26 August '37 (private letters)" and "Poor law question page 48. Estimate - 75". -- Includes table of contents end of volume. </field>
